SCREENSHOT(1)               User Commands              SCREENSHOT(1)

NAME
    screenshot - save the screen, an area or a window as a PNG file

SYNOPSIS
    screenshot [-i | -a | -w] [-p] [-t] [-d seconds] [file]

DESCRIPTION
    screenshot copies the screen from the display server at the device
    resolution and writes it to file. Without file, the image is written
    to $HOME/Pictures/screenshot-DATE-TIME.png. Without a mode the whole
    screen is saved at once.

    -i opens the capture interface over the frozen screen. The toolbar
    at the bottom selects the Selection, Screen or Window mode, toggles
    the pointer, captures and closes. In Selection mode a drag outside
    the selection draws a new one, a drag inside moves it, and the
    handles resize it. In Window mode a click chooses a window. The keys
    S, C and W select the modes, P toggles the pointer, Enter, Space and
    Print Screen capture, and Escape cancels.

    -a selects an area: a drag draws it and releasing the button saves
    it. Space switches to the window under the pointer, which a click
    saves. Escape cancels.

    A window is saved alone with its shadow on a transparent
    background, also where other windows cover it.

KEYS
    Print Screen             the capture interface (screenshot -i)
    Shift+Print Screen       the whole screen (screenshot -t)
    Super+Shift+3            the whole screen (screenshot -t)
    Super+Shift+4            an area (screenshot -a)
    Super+Shift+5            the capture interface (screenshot -i)

OPTIONS
    -i           open the capture interface
    -a           select an area
    -w           save the active window
    -p           include the pointer (not in window images)
    -t           show a thumbnail of the image in the bottom right
                 corner for five seconds; a click opens the file.
                 -i and -a show it always
    -d seconds   wait before the copy

    The path of the written file is printed on standard output. A
    cancelled capture prints nothing and exits with status 1.

EXAMPLES
    screenshot
    screenshot -d 5 /home/desktop.png
    screenshot -w /home/window.png
    screenshot -i

SEE ALSO
    gzip(1)
